WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.630
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome to the program
today, Brian Karem, who's an award winning

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:09.640
journalist and author of the new book Free
The Press, The Death of American Journalism

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.620
and How to Revive It.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:12.620
Great.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:13.620
Great having you on, Brian.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.620
I appreciate it.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:15.620
Speaker 4: Thanks.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:16.620
Pleasure being here.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:17.620
Speaker 1: So, OK.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:22.509
I mean, when did the death of American journalism
start with?

00:00:22.509 --> 00:00:26.179
Was there a particular period where we can
start to see this demise?

00:00:26.179 --> 00:00:27.470
Speaker 4: Sure.

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:33.680
I blame Richard Nixon and Roger Ailes, both
camps of the devil who one came into office

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:39.390
and, you know, committed as many crimes as
he could and the other attached to him and

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:44.760
eventually spawned Mitch McConnell and Fox
News.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:51.430
So those two together began our journey in
that it was Ronald Reagan who actually implemented

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:56.690
the first seeds of planted the seeds of destruction
and every.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:01.180
Every president in the last 40 years has had
their hand in doing it, some more than others,

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.180
some less.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:06.990
Speaker 1: And when we say the death of journalism,
I mean, in a literal sense, we have media

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.649
outlets, we have people called reporters in
all of these things.

00:01:09.649 --> 00:01:10.649
So like, what?

00:01:10.649 --> 00:01:11.649
What are you talking about?

00:01:11.649 --> 00:01:12.899
What is it that has that has degenerated?

00:01:12.899 --> 00:01:18.889
Speaker 4: Well, the term for one, you know,
to be a reporter, you have to at least have

00:01:18.889 --> 00:01:20.859
at least one copy editor.

00:01:20.859 --> 00:01:23.479
Otherwise you just some idiot spouting your
opinion.

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:25.049
And it's OK.

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:27.670
I'm all fond of, you know, opinions.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:32.679
But to be a journalist, you have to vet facts
and the process of how we do that is broken

00:01:32.679 --> 00:01:34.869
down over the years, how we do it.

00:01:34.869 --> 00:01:36.580
The institution is falling apart.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:38.170
Look, there's vast media.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:39.509
The biggest problem.

00:01:39.509 --> 00:01:45.959
The underlying largest problem in the United
States with journalism is a lack of community

00:01:45.959 --> 00:01:46.959
journalism.

00:01:46.959 --> 00:01:52.020
There are vast informational deserts, news
deserts in this country, thousands of communities

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.750
where there is no local news.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:58.349
And until we fix that problem, we can't fix
the bigger problem.

00:01:58.349 --> 00:02:04.529
The smaller problem has to be fixed with investing
in community newspapers, giving tax breaks

00:02:04.529 --> 00:02:06.999
to small business owners.

00:02:06.999 --> 00:02:09.479
You know what's wrong with a $100 rebate on
your taxes?

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:14.890
If you simply if you can prove you subscribe
to a newspaper or a radio station or something,

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:16.700
that's that's where you start.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:17.700
Because.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.830
And look, governments over the last 40 years,
state, local and federal have gotten rid of

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:26.890
public notice ads, and those are probably
one of the key things that you need to build

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:27.890
newspapers.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:33.320
In fact, those those public notice ads provide
transparency on government.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:36.060
Everybody can pick it up and go, Oh, look,
here's in the states of the City Council's

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:41.140
meeting Friday, and salesmen would use those
and lawyers would use them to find clients

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:43.460
and to find people to relate with in the community.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:47.850
And all of that help build our communities
at the national level.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:51.710
We divide our communities by going, you know,
right or left.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:55.470
But at the local level, everybody wants a
paved street.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:58.610
Everybody wants their, you know, the water
to work, the lights, the work.

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:03.040
You know, the schools to work, hospitals,
police, all of those things that we have it,

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.660
you know, Hey, why is that stoplight not working?

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:08.520
We show up at a City Council meeting and find
out.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:13.700
But all of those things have kind of broken
down over the years because of a lack of local

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:14.980
and community newspapers.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:16.590
And until we fix that.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:21.930
And by the way, that's where most national
story start is with community journalism.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.610
They start there.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:28.830
And until we invest in community journalism,
we're not going to solve our problems.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:29.830
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:33.790
So I mean, there's so many different areas
that are factors here, and I'll just name

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:34.790
some of them.

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:37.710
And then maybe you could tell us whether these
are things that could be solved individually

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:39.600
or whether they're really all part of the
same thing.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:40.600
So you've got.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:41.600
OK.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.450
On the one hand, you've got the internet,
which has created essentially this model where

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:54.819
at very little cost, you can essentially rewrite
and do reporting by phone rather than doing

00:03:54.819 --> 00:04:00.500
real investigative journalism that has put
pressure on newspapers and other other media

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:06.710
outlets to say, Well, let's let, let's divest
from the expensive investigative journalists

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:07.860
and instead do this thing.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:08.860
So that's one thing.

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:09.860
Let me mention a couple of others.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:10.860
Speaker 4: All right.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:11.860
Well, you address them one by one.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:12.860
Oh, OK.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:14.350
So I don't forget it in that regard.

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:16.489
The internet is a great thing.

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:22.370
What newspapers and television and radio and
traditional media did wrong was to get tied

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:27.990
into the perpetual news cycle so that we can
vet facts and people look.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:32.610
People scream to the left and the right that
we're that we're biased, you know, conservatively

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:33.660
or biased.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:37.070
Liberals know we're biased towards money,
right?

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:38.070
Constriction of the business.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:39.070
We go where the money is.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:40.070
Yes.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:44.300
You end up with news that you want rather
than news that you need.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:47.780
And that's the danger of that was a management
problem.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:53.330
And that's because we have fewer experienced
people in this business anymore who can fend

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:55.740
off the the need to go first.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:56.860
Oh, they're showing up.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.880
Like we have to do is take a look at the Steele
dossier, for example.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:00.880
All right.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:05.120
The Steele dossier came out in the very first
report said not vetted.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:06.780
This is not vetted information.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:10.040
And yet everyone ran with it as if it were
right.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:11.040
The problem?

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:15.300
So the internet isn't a problem, it's a solution
with the right people behind it.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:16.690
Speaker 1: Without a doubt so.

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:21.220
So that's one piece of the technological factors
that have changed things.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:23.970
Second, we look at some other countries.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:26.880
There's a fee everybody pays towards public
broadcasting.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:31.940
For example, you talk about what about $100
tax credit if you subscribe to this or that.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:37.560
We also then have the entire debate around,
Hey, you get a basic cable package and some

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:41.870
number of bucks is going to Fox News or whatever.

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:48.950
Even if you don't want it to and so people
are paying often for so-called news that they

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:50.430
don't even actually want.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:54.260
So there's that structural issue that needs
to be looked at as well, it seems to me.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:56.150
Speaker 4: Well, the problem there is.

00:05:56.150 --> 00:06:01.710
You need to reinstitute the fairness doctrine
so that you know what has happened in a recent

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:06.760
press conference, President Biden referred
to it as informational alleys that we go into

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:11.390
alleys where we hear what we want to hear
and not what we need to hear.

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:13.820
And I call it in the book Informational Silos.

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:18.540
We're all kind of siloed into our own existence,
and we never hear what anybody else is saying.

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:19.540
Sure.

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:23.130
Well, the Fairness Doctrine helped increase
public news.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:26.419
I mean, it helped spawn the news industry.

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:31.190
And so when you reinstitute a fairness doctrine,
so you have to listen to at least listen to

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:34.320
the other side on matters of import.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:37.139
Then perhaps you can break down some of those
informational silos.

00:06:37.139 --> 00:06:42.620
Speaker 1: From what I've read, though, the
the fairness doctrine, as it previously existed,

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:46.360
would essentially have no teeth in them in
an age where so much is.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:47.400
I mean, you need something different.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:49.180
You need something different because of the
internet, right?

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:51.890
Speaker 4: Well, and that's one of the things
I say in the book is you need to have a blue

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:53.260
ribbon commission to look at it.

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:55.080
You need to institute the fairness doctrine.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:07:02.260
But you need people in this industry and lawmakers
to assess how we can do it, you know, so that

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:03.650
it makes a difference.

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:04.900
The real problem?

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:07.430
The fairness doctrine never really had teeth.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:09.590
I mean, never did.

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:14.199
No one was ever lost a, you know, a license
because they didn't adhere to the fairness

00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:15.199
doctrine.

00:07:15.199 --> 00:07:19.130
It's just that it kind of gave us all a guardrail
that we could all aim for.

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:26.240
And so public service programing, public affairs
programing, news all was spawned by that.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:30.760
It started in 1949 under the Truman administration,
and people said, Yeah, OK.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:35.100
You know, they said, we're going to sell you
the airwaves, but for one hour a day or whatever,

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.510
you got to do public news.

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:41.760
And so that was kind of without any, you know,
real consequence to it.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:43.120
People fell in line.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:44.120
You need that today.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:48.639
You need at least a guardrail and then discuss
how you can make the guard real solid.

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:50.169
But yeah, it doesn't.

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:51.370
Yeah, it won't work.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:52.610
You can't just go.

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:53.780
All right.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:55.860
Reinstate the fairness doctrine as it existed.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:00.199
That won't work, but you need some form of
fairness doctrine to make sure that and make

00:08:00.199 --> 00:08:01.380
sure that it does work.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:07.380
Speaker 1: What about the socio cultural shift
that has taken place in terms of attitudes

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:08.850
towards news?

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:10.600
And this includes two pieces.

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:15.460
One is lack of media literacy, where people
can't even tell whether they're watching news

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:16.460
or opinion.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:19.570
So that's one side people not really understanding
what it is that they're watching.

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:22.790
The other side is this sort of contempt, and
we could.

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:28.940
I'm curious to hear from you how responsible
Trump was for this or others about if it's

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:35.370
adversarial and you don't like it, it's fake
or they're lying or they're biased or whatever.

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:41.260
To what degree is that change in the attitude
towards what news is a problem here?

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:45.899
Speaker 4: Well, taking your last one first
Trump was a symptom, not a cause.

00:08:45.899 --> 00:08:52.451
Trump is a con man who used existing differences
and division and further exploited them, as

00:08:52.451 --> 00:08:53.451
he told.

00:08:53.451 --> 00:08:56.660
You know, I can't remember who he told, but
he said, Look, I tell people that you're the

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:58.870
fake news, so they don't believe you, right?

00:08:58.870 --> 00:08:59.899
You know, don't.

00:08:59.899 --> 00:09:00.940
That's old.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.740
That's really an old tactic, you know, embodied
in the statement.

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:06.230
Don't shoot the messenger.

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:08.460
You know, we're just giving it to your about.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:09.500
You don't like it.

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:10.500
Too bad.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:11.500
Go clean it up.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:14.570
It's not upon us to say now we've lost.

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:20.440
I think to your first part, what we've lost
over the years is there's a lack of institutional

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:21.440
knowledge.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:25.250
And because of that lack of institutional
knowledge caused by the constriction in our

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:29.260
business and that constriction in our business
was caused by the fact that when I first got

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:33.300
in this business, there were maybe two dozen
companies that ran 80 percent of what you

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:39.040
see reader here today, there's five that commandeer
about 90 percent of what you see or hear,

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:40.040
right?

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:44.810
And that's because we removed the restrictions
on ownership.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:48.990
And so what happened was is larger and larger
companies began to buy each other up and as

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:50.670
they did, bottom line counted more.

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:56.500
There were fewer reporters, like if you owned
a media conglomerate that used to be comprised

00:09:56.500 --> 00:10:02.070
of four different or five different or a hundred
different newspapers who all had reporters

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:06.490
in Washington, D.C. for their specific newspaper,
you would go, Well, I can save money by just

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:08.490
having one reporter there, right?

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:13.420
Got rid of a lot of jobs and experience, and
it became more popular.

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:17.050
When I first got in this business, you had
to have three to five years experience working

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:20.370
as a beat reporter somewhere in a small market.

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:22.550
So you understood what it was to be a reporter.

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:23.550
Right?

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:28.450
Today, they hire you straight out of school
and will hire you and put you in the White

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:31.940
House with no experience at all.

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:37.800
And therefore, we lose our credibility because
what you end up with are reporters without

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:42.860
experience who are more, you know, they're
more susceptible to the access journalism.

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:44.760
Oh, I won't be able to get on Air Force One.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:46.560
Oh, I won't be allowed into this room.

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:49.899
Oh, I won't be able to talk to these people
if I do this.

00:10:49.899 --> 00:10:55.720
So that's led to what is a disgustingly low
level of real report.

00:10:55.720 --> 00:11:00.640
Awarding it in the United States, and it's
only going to get worse if we don't fix it,

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:06.279
you know, it was in 1958 that Edward R. Murrow,
appearing before the Radio Television News

00:11:06.279 --> 00:11:12.300
Directors Association, warned us that if we
don't clean up our act, we are destined to

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:16.090
fall for nothing but slogans and propaganda
in the future.

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:18.770
And by God, yeah, we nailed it.

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:21.660
Speaker 1: So what's the lowest hanging fruit?

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:25.920
Because you've got the media literacy piece,
you've got the media consolidation and lack

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:28.440
of enforcement of antitrust piece.

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:34.140
You've got regulatory FCC net neutrality,
fairness doctrine aspect of this.

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:35.710
There's all these different things.

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:39.080
What's the lowest hanging fruit that would
have the greatest impact, most quickly?

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:45.640
Speaker 4: Community newspapers invest in
them and give tax breaks for them and encourage

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:46.790
them.

00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:50.990
And that's where you start, because that's
where you build reporters.

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:53.029
Some of the best reporters look.

00:11:53.029 --> 00:11:55.220
Why did Walter Cronkite have so much gravitas?

00:11:55.220 --> 00:12:00.790
In 1965, I think it was when he went to Vietnam
and he developed.

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:06.140
He put together a documentary which was all
fact based, and then at the end of it, he

00:12:06.140 --> 00:12:07.220
offered an opinion.

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:10.310
It's not unusual for reporters to offer opinions.

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:11.370
He offered one, he said.

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:13.510
It's obvious to this report that we're not
going to win.

00:12:13.510 --> 00:12:15.360
We've got to negotiate our way out of it.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:18.670
And LBJ said, I've lost Middle America, I've
lost Cronkite.

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:20.050
I'm doomed.

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:22.500
I got, you know, I won't run for reelection.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:26.460
Why did people why do people respect that
opinion?

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:31.700
Well, because Walter Cronkite had the gravitas
to to be able to say it.

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:33.370
He had covered World War Two.

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:35.570
He was a radio beat reporter.

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:39.440
He had experience upon experience, upon experience.

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:44.030
What anchor today on any news show has that
what?

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:45.410
What would tell it?

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:50.560
What newspaper writer today has the gravitas
of an H.L.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:51.560
Mencken?

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:56.070
We don't have that because we have lost institutional
knowledge.

00:12:56.070 --> 00:13:00.910
When you when it costs too much to have a
reporter around, they find other ways to make

00:13:00.910 --> 00:13:01.910
money.

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:02.910
Because guess what?

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:05.620
By the time you're in your mid thirties or
forties and you got a family, you can't exist

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:08.720
on what they're going to pay you in this business
Speaker 1: as a reporter.

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:15.160
So if I understand the community newspaper,
community media outlet piece, it's good because

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:18.010
number one, it creates more diversity of opinion.

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:22.779
It's good because number two, it trains reporters
to be ready to go to the national level.

00:13:22.779 --> 00:13:27.440
Number three, it's good because financially
it's it's dividing money up among more institutions

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:30.620
like there's a bunch of different ways in
which that is a positive thing.

00:13:30.620 --> 00:13:31.620
Right?

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:32.620
Speaker 4: I agree.

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:34.589
And yeah, yeah, I agree because I wrote it.

00:13:34.589 --> 00:13:40.350
But but the and then there are other things
you don't like the reinstitution.

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:44.579
I think you need a national shield law to
protect reporters so they don't go to jail.

00:13:44.579 --> 00:13:46.910
They have confidential sources.

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:50.910
As a reporter who was jailed four times, I
can tell you it's not fun.

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:56.450
And then the most controversial thing in the
thing that's that hurts and bites the most

00:13:56.450 --> 00:14:01.630
for businesses, you know, using antitrust
legislation to bust up the big monopolies.

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:02.630
Right?

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:07.400
But it has to be done in some respects, somehow,
and that's why you need a blue ribbon commission

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:09.040
to decide how to do it.

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:13.330
You have to have more reporters on the planet.

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:16.279
There are twice the number of people on this
planet.

00:14:16.279 --> 00:14:18.620
Today is on the day that I was born.

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:21.880
And probably about half the number of reporters.

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:22.880
That's a problem.

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:23.880
Right.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:26.760
And it's illustrated best by a place I used
to work.

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:32.040
LAREDO, Texas When I worked there in the 1980s,
there were a hundred thousand people.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:35.630
There were three television stations that
broadcast in English.

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:40.110
One that broadcast or to the broadcast in
Spanish.

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:44.810
There were two daily newspapers in English
and two in Spanish.

00:14:44.810 --> 00:14:48.700
There were probably four or five radio stations
that broadcast news.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:54.690
Now that was thirty five years, almost thirty
eight years ago and a hundred thousand people

00:14:54.690 --> 00:15:02.120
today there are three hundred thousand people
and there's one television station and one

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:03.120
newspaper, right?

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:04.120
Right.

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:05.120
That's it.

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:06.120
There is your problem in a nutshell.

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:11.690
Speaker 1: Hey, we had I'm going to give you
two examples to kind of frame this next question.

00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:13.370
You know, you tell me what you think.

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:18.970
Under Trump, there was this famous incident
where he flipped out on CNN's Jim Acosta and

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:24.000
then had his press credential pulled after
Jim Acosta insisted on asking a question and

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:27.590
didn't immediately turn turn the mike over
when they tried to take it from him.

00:15:27.590 --> 00:15:30.660
Last week, Joe Biden, or maybe it was this
week, who knows?

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:36.190
Referred to Fox reporter Peter Doocy as a
son of a bitch, and he may have been knowingly

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:38.450
on the mic, or maybe not or whatever.

00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:40.970
What those incidents are.

00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:44.330
They equally concerning in your mind?

00:15:44.330 --> 00:15:45.420
Neither.

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:46.420
Both one.

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:47.420
The other.

00:15:47.420 --> 00:15:50.320
What do they mean about the relationship between
federal government and the media?

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:55.639
Speaker 4: First of all, Jim Acosta is a First
Amendment warrior, and I love it.

00:15:55.639 --> 00:16:01.010
And secondly, there were two people that had
their press passes pulled during the Trump

00:16:01.010 --> 00:16:02.420
administration.

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:06.060
Jim was the first, I was the second and I
had to battle the S.O.B..

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:07.690
I'll use the word son of a bitch.

00:16:07.690 --> 00:16:10.400
I had a battle with Trump that S.O.B.

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:15.170
three times and I beat him three times in
court to keep my press pass.

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:16.170
Right?

00:16:16.170 --> 00:16:20.050
And Jim fought him to keep his press pass.

00:16:20.050 --> 00:16:22.600
That is what concerns me.

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:29.120
I had to stand up in a in a court, a federal
court with my attorney, Ted Boutrous, who

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:33.339
was also a great First Amendment lawyer and
represented both me and Jim.

00:16:33.339 --> 00:16:34.339
Yep.

00:16:34.339 --> 00:16:39.430
And it was it was Ted who stood up and said,
you know, the administration said they had

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:43.820
to ban my press pass because if they didn't
come down on me and people like me that they

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:46.930
were afraid that reporters would run through
the halls mooning people.

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:51.070
Honest to God, they said that was what they
said.

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:56.730
And imagine having to defend that, taking
your time out as a reporter.

00:16:56.730 --> 00:17:00.270
I had to sit in court for that.

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:01.639
Me being called an S.O.B.

00:17:01.639 --> 00:17:03.820
by a president, I don't care.

00:17:03.820 --> 00:17:10.740
I, you know, Peter still gets it, and I know
Peter and I have nothing against any question

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:14.180
that anyone wants to ask in that briefing
room.

00:17:14.180 --> 00:17:17.310
There's no such thing as bad questions, only
bad answers.

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:19.020
God bless you for asking anything.

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:20.970
What may not be the question I'd ask?

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:26.380
I may think, Well, that's kind of shallow,
but it's it's always the answers that are

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:27.870
more concerning for us.

00:17:27.870 --> 00:17:34.990
You know, there's the old story told of during
Betty Ford's days when there was a group of

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:40.150
reporters, including one fresh off the Choo-Choo
who saw her and a gaggle and the young reporters

00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:43.220
said, Hey to your kids, did your kids ever
smoke pot?

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:46.840
And everybody thought, Well, that's the stupidest
question in the world, right up until the

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:50.380
time where Betty Ford said yes, right?

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:51.700
And he was brilliant.

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:53.120
All right.

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:57.700
You, you may think a question is stupid and
reflect upon and go, Well, that's a stupid

00:17:57.700 --> 00:17:58.780
question.

00:17:58.780 --> 00:18:00.760
But it's not the questions that matter.

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:02.299
It's the answers.

00:18:02.299 --> 00:18:07.210
So I don't care if the president wants to
call me a dumb S.O.B. or anybody else.

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:12.380
And so he gave him access, and Peters can
still ask his questions.

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:18.809
So there's a difference there, a big difference
between having your press pass yanked, having

00:18:18.809 --> 00:18:26.700
to go to court to defend it, having a president
fundamentally unknowing or uncaring of what

00:18:26.700 --> 00:18:33.540
the First Amendment means and what a press
is there for a president and a president who

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:34.540
says, Hey, he's a dumb S.O.B..

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:40.780
I mean, I heard presidents say that pretty
much about every reporter in the White House.

00:18:40.780 --> 00:18:43.250
So that's not they're not equal.

00:18:43.250 --> 00:18:44.250
They're not equitable.

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:45.250
They're not.

00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:47.380
There's no way they're on the same level.

00:18:47.380 --> 00:18:52.790
And if you think differently, I invite you
to sit in court with me or Jim and fight to

00:18:52.790 --> 00:18:55.690
keep our damn press passes and keep doing
our job.

00:18:55.690 --> 00:18:59.540
Speaker 1: Yeah, yeah, that is the exact distinction
I made after it happened.

00:18:59.540 --> 00:19:04.340
The new book is free, the press, the death
of American journalism and how to revive it.

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:06.290
The book's author is Brian Karem.

00:19:06.290 --> 00:19:08.270
Brian, really appreciate your time today.

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:09.220
Speaker 4: Sure, anytime.

